\f1\fs26 When Donald Dunning-Kruger was president of the United Free States of Atlantia, and Vietnesian President Sucarn served words of peace and goodwill, with a side-salad of excuses and caveats:\
\'93As part of our peace-keeping initiatives in our southern territorial waters, a small flotilla of our craft regrettably ran aground on the shores of Ulyssia. I assure the good people of our dearest neighbour, our intentions are peaceful. In the interest of bilateral relations, especially the safety of both peoples, we accept our unfortunate mistake, and will rescue our service folk on conclusion of exercises.\'94\
The 
\f2\i small flotilla, 
\f1\i0 it transpired, represented a significant, specialised chunk of the Vietnesian Navy, all trained in the art of lifting gold, uranium and iron ore from whichever sovereign soil they happened upon running aground.\
After receiving intelligence, such as it was, of the Vietnesian boosting Ulyssian natural resources, the Prime Minister of Ulyssia, the Right Honorable Spot Muddleham, realised he must invoke treaty, and contact, \'93that moron Dunning-Kruger.\'94\
Muddleham knew he was inviting the devil to dinner, but he had no option. And so he crafted words of diplomacy for the Atlantian ear: \'93Mr President, We are in deep shit here. Can you bail us out?\'94 \
Dunning-Kruger exploited many moments of tension before he spoke, or rather, it took a fucking eon for him to reply with, \'93We of the UFSA are mindful of our friends in the south.\'94 He spoke slowly, reinforcing the rumour he recited everything from a tele-prompter. \'93I am also mindful of my responsibilities to my fellow Atlantians.\'94\
Muddleham scratched his balding melon. \'93You\'92re saying you can\'92t afford it.\'94\
A flash of animal anger ignited the wispy rendering of Dunning-Kruger\'92s soft, entitled face as it hovered on the other side of Muddleham desk. Then, after another interminable wait, in which rumours flourished that Dunning-Kruger\'92s SecDef vets all his utterances, he said, \'93We are currently fighting infractions in ten theatres.\'94\
